{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-12T00:23:30.260Z","sessionId":"904c52d9-f200-4552-8ce5-05e400952efa","content":"<cross-session-message from=\"uds:<socket>\" from-session=\"local_beb3cc8c-e162-46cc-bd0a-f870e8717ebd\" from-name=\"Flare Manager(founders CTO)\" from-mode=\"prompting\">\nLive bug 2, from the founder's friend on the real Flare at 8:22 PM. It ranks with the photo fix, ahead of the automations.\n\nThe friend typed \"After 10min, a quick visit works\". The readback said \"Got it: after 10 AM. Right?\" with the chip \"After 10:00 AM\". The friend caught it on the readback, but it's wrong in two ways:\n\n1. \"10min\" with no space was read as a clock time. Read these as a delay from now (\"can come in 10 min\"): 10min, 10mins, 10m, 10 min, 10 minutes, in 10, after 10 min, give me 10, half an hour, an hour.\n2. A bare hour with no am or pm took the morning, at 8:22 PM. Pick the next upcoming time relative to the Flare's own time (8:22 PM plus \"after 10\" means 10 PM), and never a time that has already passed.\n\nAlso:\n3. \"a quick visit works\" was silently dropped. If there's a short-stay rule, map it (\"quick\", \"a quick visit\", \"for a bit\", \"just stopping by\"). If there isn't, the readback must say what it didn't understand (\"I didn't catch: a quick visit works\") instead of dropping it. Readbacks never silently drop words.\n4. Tests:\n   - add the friend's exact sentence plus the variants above to hard_20, with the time of day as an input (8:22 PM, 9 AM, 11:50 PM)\n   - re-run blind_40 and report it with no regression\n   - add a property test: the resolved time is never earlier than the Flare's start\n5. Readback copy for a delay: \"Got it: you can come in 10 min. Right?\"\n\nReport the before/after on the friend's sentence and the blind_40 score when it's live. With the friend's OK, this is a good concrete item for \"What I got wrong\" in the post.\n</cross-session-message>"}
